cartridge "STEEPLE RUN/TONY BRANTNER/1987"

' STEEPLE RUN
' A ColecoVision game based on "Steeplechase" by Tony Brantner, Ahoy!
' August 1987 (Commodore 64, machine code). Made for Amy Studio by Claude
' (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a byte-for-byte port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/ml.dis), the game is rebuilt for the ColecoVision:
' smooth sprites, hills that scroll slower than the turf (as the C64's
' split screen did), tile scenery redrawn, sounds and a title tune.
'
' The game: two horses race in two lanes, one above the other, over a
' course of 1, 2 or 3 miles. Push right to drive your horse on, left to
' rein it in; let go and it slows down. A button jumps. Hedges, rails,
' walls and water come at you: touch one and your horse is held to a crawl
' until it is past. The first to finish wins; the best time for each
' course length is kept until the power goes off.
'
' From the original (orig/ml.dis): speed 0-46 in 256ths, +10 a pass with
' right, -16 with left, -3 when let go, never 47 or more; a step of one
' pixel every 50 - speed passes, so the horse creeps at speed 0 and the
' last few points of speed count most; a gallop frame every 8 steps; a
' hundredth of a mile every 128 steps; the jump lasts a fixed time (the
' same at any speed), so a fast horse jumps further; an obstacle touched
' holds the speed at 10 while they overlap; the obstacles come at random
' (four kinds), at most a few at a time; the race ends when one horse has
' gone the course's distance; the time runs in tenths; the best time of
' each course (1, 2, 3) is kept; the course chosen with the joystick on
' the title; the horses trot in from the left before the start.
'
' Liberties: speeds are per frame (the C64 ran its loop about 650 times a
' second: right adds 98/256 a frame, left takes 157, letting go 29; a step
' is 2560 / (50 - speed) 256ths of a pixel a frame); the jump lasts 48
' frames, 16 pixels high at the top, and it starts on a press (the C64
' jumped again at once while the button was held: holding it kept the
' horse in the air for ever); a horse can only start another jump 8 frames
' after landing, and it can't be driven on in the air (it slows as if let
' go), so jumping all the time no longer clears every obstacle; obstacles
' are 16 pixels wide and come 112 to 367 pixels apart (the C64 drew some
' double width and spaced them by 256-pixel turns); the hitboxes are
' boxes (the horse's legs, the obstacle's height), not the C64's sprite
' collisions; the C64 raced two players or one horse alone; here one
' player races the clock, or the second horse is the computer's; a
' 3-2-1-GO count before the start; the C64 played a note per stride, here
' hoof clops.
'
' Controls: joystick only. Right drives on, left reins in, either side
' button jumps. Player 1 uses controller 1 and races in the top lane,
' player 2 controller 2 in the bottom lane (one player: either
' controller). On the title, up and down choose a line, left, right or a
' button change it, a button on START starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers.
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs steeple-print-digits-first-and-last, steeple-word-table-u16-index removed 2026-11-01.

